{"created": "2025-10-16T20:31:53.520738+00:00", "modified": "2025-10-29T18:58:28.746769+00:00", "id": 17372599, "conceptrecid": "17372598", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.17372599", "conceptdoi": "10.5281/zenodo.17372598", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.17372599", "metadata": {"title": "Testing Non-Minimal Gravitational Coupling of Neutrinos via Entropy-Production Mechanism: Multi-Messenger Evidence and Validation with Post-2018 Data", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.17372599", "publication_date": "2025-10-16", "description": "<p>We propose that neutrinos exhibit anomalous gravitational coupling arising from an entropy-production mechanism in the light-gravity interaction sector. Within a non-minimal coupling framework, we introduce a modified action where the photon field coupling to spacetime curvature generates entropy, with neutrinos emerging as the entropic channel of this irreversible process. This predicts effective zero gravitational coupling for neutrinos (&xi;_&nu; &asymp; 0), contrasting with standard minimal coupling (&xi;_&nu; = 1).</p>\n<p>Key Results: - SN 1987A temporal entropy: S/S_max = 0.80 &plusmn; 0.04 (4.8&sigma; above thermal) - IceCube 12-year isotropy: &chi;&sup2; = 0.01 (3.0&sigma; rejection of MW lensing) - Six-source curvature correlation: r = 0.81, p = 0.05 (significant) - Combined Bayesian analysis: BF = 72 (4.6&sigma; strong evidence) - Cosmological CAMB analysis: &Delta;&chi;&sup2; = -1.8 (mild preference for NMC) We propose definitive tests via multi-messenger observations of gravitationally lensed transients, predicting systematic neutrino early arrival by photon Shapiro delay (10-100 ms), achievable with IceCube-Gen2 + Einstein Telescope + LSST by 2030-2035.</p>\n<p>Significance: This work presents the first comprehensive observational test of species-dependent gravitational coupling, with profound implications for quantum gravity, cosmology, and the nature of neutrinos as quasi-particles rather than fundamental fields.</p>", "access_ri